course: dictionary
lesson: 15148
title: Scholarship: money or study?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A scholarship is usually money. A school or university gives it to a pupil or student who is very good at something, or who needs help to pay for their education. «Mia won a scholarship to her new school because her maths was excellent.» The clue is won and maths. Winning something and being excellent at maths tell you the scholarship is a reward, and it helps pay for school.
explain: The word can also mean serious study and learning, the work of scholars. «The monks spent years in quiet scholarship, copying old books.» Here there is no money at all. The clue is copying old books, which is careful learning. So the same word can name a prize or a kind of hard work, and the sentence tells you which one.
explain: Check the meaning by putting it into the sentence. If «Mia won a scholarship» means «Mia won money to help pay for school», it still makes sense. Then ask: do the other words talk about money, or about learning? That question will tell you which meaning the writer chose.
fact: A scholarship is money given to a pupil or student, often because they are very good at something or need help.
fact: A full scholarship pays all the costs of study.
fact: A sports scholarship is given to a student because they are good at a sport, for example at a university in the US.
fact: Scholarship can also mean serious study and learning, the work of scholars.
fact: «On a scholarship» means studying somewhere with money given to help pay for it.
exercise: «Mia won a scholarship to her new school because her maths was excellent.» What does scholarship mean here? | A long holiday from lessons | A prize for the best dancer in the class | A new bag for school | *Money given to help pay for school, because the pupil is very good
exercise: «The monks spent years in quiet scholarship, copying old books.» What does scholarship mean here? | A type of church | *Serious study and learning | Writing very fast | Money given to a monk
exercise: Tom says, «I hope to get a full scholarship.» What does full scholarship suggest? | *Money that pays all the costs | A scholarship with a lot of homework | Only half of the school fees are paid | A scholarship only for swimming
test: «Ali studies at the school on a scholarship.» What does «on a scholarship» mean? | He is studying a sport | *He is there with money given to help pay for it | He has lots of books in his bag | He is very tall for his age
test: «The scholarship of the ancient monks saved old books for us.» Which meaning fits best? | A building where monks slept | A holiday for the monks | *Serious study and learning | Money paid to the monks
exam: «Ravi got a sports scholarship, so his university fees were paid.» What does scholarship mean here? | A test he had to pass | A school trip to the sports centre | *Money given to pay for study, because of his sport | Serious study of old books
